2. All 156 session-condition pairs produce aligned arrays
3. Epoch alignment passes validation (cross-session dims match)

### How to Run (PROPOSED INTERFACE)

```bash
# Full-sequence epoch recovery for all sessions
python scripts/recover_full_sequence_epochs.py \
  --data-root D:\workspace\data \
  --a4-validation reports/analysis_A4_trial_count_validation \
  --output-dir outputs/epochs_full_sequence \
  --validate-cross-session

# Single-session debugging
python scripts/recover_full_sequence_epochs.py \
  --session 230629 \
  --output-dir outputs/epochs_full_sequence \
  --verbose
```

### Expected Outputs
```
outputs/epochs_full_sequence/
├── ses230629_epochs_lfp.npy        (shape: 156, 128, 6000) — 156 trials × 128 channels
├── ses230629_epochs_spk.npy        (sparse array)
├── ses230629_epoch_metadata.json    (condition, alignment_time, trial_indices)
├── ses230630_epochs_lfp.npy
├── ...
├── validation_report.md             (cross-session alignment check)
└── validation_report.json           (metrics)
```

### Success Criteria (MUST ALL PASS)
- [ ] All 13 sessions processed without errors
- [ ] All 156 session-condition pairs produce epochs
- [ ] Cross-session dimension check: all epochs shape (N_trials, channels, 6000)
- [ ] No NaN blocks > 50ms in any LFP channel
- [ ] SPK sparse arrays: all have 0-1000 spikes/trial (realistic range)
- [ ] Baseline phase (p1) has statistically lower power than p2/p3 (sanity check)
- [ ] Omission conditions (AXAB, AAXB, AAAX) show distinct signatures in p2/p3/p4 windows

### Validation Logic

```python
# Pseudocode for validation
for session in sessions:
    for condition in conditions:
        epochs_lfp = load_epochs(session, condition)

        # Check 1: Shape consistency
        assert epochs_lfp.shape == (n_trials, 128, 6000), f"Shape mismatch {session} {condition}"

        # Check 2: NaN presence
        assert not np.isnan(epochs_lfp).any(axis=(1, 2)).max() > 0.05, "Too many NaNs"

        # Check 3: Baseline vs. stimulus power (LFP sanity)
        p1_power = np.mean(epochs_lfp[:, :, :250])  # first 250ms
        p2_power = np.mean(epochs_lfp[:, :, 250:500])
        assert p2_power > p1_power * 1.1, "p2 power should exceed baseline"

        # Check 4: Cross-session consistency
        for other_session in sessions:
            other_shape = load_epochs(other_session, condition).shape
            assert epochs_lfp.shape[0] == other_shape[0], "Trial count mismatch across sessions"
```

## Scientific Motivation

Current blocker: **"Epochs are blocked"**

Why epochs matter:
- **Figures 4–10 depend on epochs**: TFR, Granger, PAC, ghost signals, state manifolds all require aligned windows
- **Multi-session comparison requires consistency**: If sessions have different epoch structures, cross-session averaging becomes invalid
- **Omission science requires precision**: Off-by-one-sample alignment errors can destroy 30dB signal-to-noise ratios in spectral analysis

Omission-induced state perturbations are **low-frequency, broad, and sparse in time** (your doctrine: "omission broadly perturbs low-frequency cortical state"). This makes epoch alignment **critical**—a 20ms misalignment can smear 10Hz effects into noise.

